Recently, some people have suggested that it should be possible to fire
a missile weapon in any direction, not just yerbasic eight, and that some
sort of aiming procedure could be implemented.

This is not a bad idea, but I just wanted to mention that I felt an incredible
twinge of excitement when I deduced that:

	- You can protect yourself from missile attack by understanding
	  and adapting to these limitations

	- Some monsters are smart about this as well!

Like it or not, you are an at sign, and that floating eye is a lower case e.
There are lots of games that are not confined to the restrictive world of
ascii, but many do not approach the sophistication of nethack in other ways.
Nethack is my favorite game, its visual boringness notwithstanding.

Therefore, why must we always strive to make the game seem to be something
that it isn't?  Unless our goal is ultimate divorce from the iron balls and
chains of ascii (again, not a bad idea, though of course portability suffers),
why not embrace the medium and enjoy and exploit its properties? It is
comforting to know that a monster cannot employ a missile attack if
I am not suitably aligned, and satisfying to figure that out.

Chess is a good analogy:  were we to try to make chess more 'realistic' as a
battle simulation, we should allow any piece to attack any other piece
immediately adjacent to it.  Ahhh, but you say that chess is not a battle
simulation, it's a fascinating game of strategy.  Bingo.

